 ## Overview
-Single 2D Projection Voxelization is a method that produces 6-separating (tunnel-free) voxelization using only one 2D projection along the dominant axis of a triangle's normal vector [S1, unofficial]. It leverages the "thinness" property of voxelization to avoid the computational cost of multi-axis projections [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Orthographic Multi-View Voxelization](3892-orthographic-multi-view-voxelization.md) which projects along all three axes, this method projects only along the axis where the triangle is most "face-on," relying on edge-crossing tests to ensure topological correctness [S1, unofficial].
+Single 2D Projection Voxelization is a method for producing 6-separating (tunnel-free) voxel grids from planar triangles by leveraging the geometric "thinness" property [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of volumetric coverage with minimal computational overhead by requiring only a single 2D rasterization pass along the dominant axis of the triangle's normal, rather than the three orthogonal passes used in standard multi-view methods [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Orthographic Multi-View Voxelization](3892-orthographic-multi-view-voxelization.md) which projects along all three axes, this method relies on the fact that for any pixel center covered by the projection, the dominant axis projection is sufficient to determine the unique voxel in that column, handling edge cases via crosshair-segment intersection tests [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A planar triangle defined by its vertices; the target voxel grid resolution $R = (R_x, R_y, R_z)$; the scene's axis-aligned bounding box (AABB) defined by $bb_{min}$ and $bb_{max}$.
-**Outputs:** A set of occupied voxels in the 3D grid.
